[Input] About arXivLabs\nL151: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL152: \nL153: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL154: \nL155: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL156: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite50†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL157: \nL158: cite51†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite52†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL159: \nL160: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite53†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL161: cite54†About†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite58†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite59†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite60†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite61†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L162: \nL163: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing KernelQuest: Can agents write Triton kernels? (https://www.turing.com/blog/kernelquest-benchmark-ai-agents-triton-kernels)\nciteturn34view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.turing.com/blog/kernelquest-benchmark-ai-agents-triton-kernels\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 192\nL0: # Introducing KernelQuest: Can agents write Triton kernels?\nL1: \nL2: A benchmark for measuring whether AI agents can deliver correct, verifiable, expert-level GPU kernel optimization.\nL3: \nL4: cite0†Image: Turing Frontier Research Lab†images.prismic.io L5: \nL6: Turing Frontier Research Lab\nL7: \nL8: 6 min read\nL9: \nL10: September 7, 2026\nL11: \nL12: Research\nL13: \nL14: cite1†Image: Introducing KernelQuest: Can agents write Triton kernels?†images.prismic.io L15: \nL16: Table of Contents\nL17:   * cite2†The challenge: speed is easy to claim and hard to verify L18:   * cite3†Why we built KernelQuest L19:   * cite4†What KernelQuest measures L20:   * cite5†Quality is part of the benchmark, not a post-processing step L21:   * cite6†One task shows why the gates matter L22:   * cite7†Leaderboard L23:   * cite8†Failure modes L24:   * cite9†Building the evidence needed to push frontier models forward L25: The Turing Frontier Research Lab is launching cite10†KernelQuest†labs.turing.com , a benchmark designed to measure whether AI agents can perform the full job of GPU kernel optimization. KernelQuest contains 100 engineer-authored Triton tasks, each delivered as a live environment where an agent can profile a PyTorch workload, write and revise kernels, build timing probes, measure progress, and submit a final solution.\nL26: The benchmark asks a stricter question than whether an agent can beat a stopwatch. Can it produce a genuine Triton kernel that is correct, faster than a serious compiled baseline, and able to survive an adversarial verifier?\nL27: The Turing Frontier Research Lab built KernelQuest around a straightforward premise: a benchmark should reward real kernel optimization, not shortcuts that happen to score well. Our mission is to create frontier-calibrated data and benchmarks for the next generation of AI. We build benchmarks and training data that push frontier models forward in the domains where real capability matters.\nL28: Kernel optimization is one of those domains because small differences at the kernel layer compound across every training step and every served token.\nL29: Table of Contents\nL30: \nL31:   * cite2†The challenge: speed is easy to claim and hard to verify L32:   * cite3†Why we built KernelQuest L33:   * cite4†What KernelQuest measures L34:   * cite5†Quality is part of the benchmark, not a post-processing step L35:   * cite6†One task shows why the gates matter L36:   * cite7†Leaderboard L37:   * cite8†Failure modes L38:   * cite9†Building the evidence needed to push frontier models forward L39: ## The challenge: speed is easy to claim and hard to verify\nL40: \nL41: Kernel performance is where much of the cost of AI is decided. A kernel that leaves a third of the hardware idle makes training and inference more expensive. The distance between generic compiler output and expert-written code is routinely 2x or wider. Closing that gap requires architecture knowledge and a working method: profile, form a hypothesis, rewrite, measure, discard, and repeat.\n"
